---
// About page: the organisation at a glance, why it does both policy and
// teaching, the founder's own account, the people who lead it, and how it
// started. The People section replaces the old Team page.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageTop from '../components/PageTop.astro';
import SummaryList from '../components/SummaryList.astro';
import Gallery from '../components/Gallery.astro';
import '../styles/pages/about.css';
import { getCollection, render } from 'astro:content';
import { facts } from '../data/facts';

const f = facts;

// The people who lead AFG (src/content/team/). Anyone without a bio is
// listed without the button to open one.
const people = await Promise.all(
  (await getCollection('team'))
    .filter((m) => !m.data.draft)
    .sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order)
    .map(async (m) => ({
      name: m.data.name,
      first: m.data.name.split(' ')[0],
      role: m.data.role,
      Bio: m.body?.trim() ? (await render(m)).Content : null,
    }))
);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  pageClass="page-about"
  description="AI For Good is a student-run organisation in Hong Kong, started in June 2023. We write policy recommendations for the government and run free tutoring for children from underprivileged families."
>
  <PageTop vertical="關於我們">
    <h1>Policy and teaching, for the same reason.</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      AI For Good is a student-run organisation in Hong Kong, started in {f.founded.en}. We write AI and
      economic policy recommendations for the government, and run free tutoring for primary school
      children from underprivileged families.
    </p>
  </PageTop>

  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="at-a-glance">
    <div class="wrap rail">
      <div class="rail-head">
        <h2 id="at-a-glance">At a glance.</h2>
      </div>
      <SummaryList
        rows={[
          { key: 'Founded', value: `${f.founded.en}, by ${f.founders[0]}, ${f.founders[1]} and ${f.founders[2]}` },
          { key: 'Members', value: `${f.members.value} students` },
          {
            key: 'Policy',
            value: `About ${f.recsPerYear.value.replace('~', '')} recommendations a year; every Policy Address and Budget consultation since ${f.recsPerYear.since}`,
          },
          {
            key: 'Teaching',
            value: `${f.children.value} children, ${f.schools.value} schools, ${f.tutors.value} trained tutors, ${f.modules.length} modules`,
          },
          {
            key: 'Outcome',
            value: `<a href="/policy#ai-for-all">${f.aiForAll.amount.en} for AI for All</a> in the ${f.aiForAll.budget} Budget`,
          },
          {
            key: 'Partners',
            value: "Ho Man Tin Ling To Catholic School, Kennedy Town School, St. Barnabas' Home &amp; Society and 10% Organisation",
          },
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="why-both">
    <div class="wrap rail">
      <div class="rail-head">
        <h2 id="why-both">People who write policy should meet the people it affects.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lead">
          AI policy written by people who have never met the people it affects isn't good policy. The
          children we teach are the people it affects.
        </p>
        <p>
          It isn't a coincidence that we do both. Sit in a classroom in Ho Man Tin and watch a child use an
          AI tutor for the first time, and you learn things a consultation paper won't tell you: which
          children already have a tutor at home, which ones have a device, and which ones are about to fall
          further behind because of a technology everyone keeps calling an equaliser.
        </p>
        <p>
          Most of our submissions start there. Teaching shows us where the gaps are. Research tells us
          whether a gap is a policy problem. Then the submission asks the government to fix it. It works
          the other way too: what we learn writing about education and labour policy changes what we think
          is worth teaching a ten-year-old. We'd rather be the people who have been in the room than the
          people who have only read about it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Justin's own account, in the first person and left in his words.
       The voice shift from "we" to "I" is deliberate and signed. -->
  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="why-i-started">
    <div class="wrap rail">
      <div class="rail-head">
        <h2 id="why-i-started">Why I started it.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h3>Why the policy work</h3>
        <p>
          AI arrived in Hong Kong faster than the city could decide who it was for. I watched it show up in
          my school, then in my friends' internships, then in the government's plans, and the pattern was
          always the same: the people who already had the most access got it first. Nobody was choosing
          that. It was just what happened when nobody was asked to choose. I had spent two years learning
          how public money gets allocated and who ends up bearing the cost of a decision, and it seemed
          absurd to sit on that while the decisions were being made a few kilometres away. So we started
          writing to the people making them.
        </p>
        <h3>Why the teaching</h3>
        <p>
          This started as a group of us at school teaching each other AI, mostly by playing with it. We
          built things that didn't work and a few that did, and it was obvious how much you could get out
          of these tools once someone showed you the first step. Then it became obvious who wasn't getting
          shown. The children with the most to gain from AI were the least likely to ever be handed it,
          and that gap wasn't going to close on its own. So we took what we'd been doing for fun in a
          classroom and brought it to schools where nobody else was offering it.
        </p>
        <p class="signed">Justin Lo, Founder &amp; Director</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="people">
    <div class="wrap rail">
      <div class="rail-head">
        <h2 id="people">Run entirely by students.</h2>
        <p>Our members work on policy research and tutoring. These are the people who lead it.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <!-- Each bio opens under its name: a native <details>, so it works
             from the keyboard and without JavaScript. -->
        <ul class="people">
          {
            people.map(({ name, first, role, Bio }) => (
              <li class="person">
                <h3>{name}</h3>
                <p class="person-role">{role}</p>
                {Bio && (
                  <details>
                    <summary>About {first}</summary>
                    <div class="prose">
                      <Bio />
                    </div>
                  </details>
                )}
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <div class="people-photos">
          <Gallery
            photos={[
              {
                name: 'team-classroom.jpg',
                alt: 'Six AI For Good members in a classroom at Ho Man Tin Ling To Catholic School, after a session.',
                caption: 'In a classroom at Ho Man Tin Ling To Catholic School, after a session.',
              },
              {
                name: 'team-outside-school.jpg',
                alt: 'Five AI For Good members outside Ho Man Tin Ling To Catholic School.',
                caption: 'Outside the school.',
              },
            ]}
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="how-it-started">
    <div class="wrap rail">
      <div class="rail-head">
        <h2 id="how-it-started">How it started.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          AI For Good was founded by <strong>Justin Lo</strong>, <strong>Ka Hei Ng</strong> and{' '}
          <strong>Oscar Zheng</strong>. <strong>David Dong</strong> and <strong>Jackie Xue</strong> joined
          later. We started outside school, as an independent think tank rather than a school club. Since
          then we've grown and started working with schools across Hong Kong, which is how the tutoring
          programme reaches the children it does.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
